## Dr. Jain's main point

Transformation from 1-7 to 0-100 is linear (but he meant affine, not linear). 

$$T(x) = \frac{100}{x_{(n)} - x_{(1)}} (x-x_{(1)})$$
$$Z(x) = \frac{x - \mu_x}{\sigma_X}$$
Dr. Jain's claim:

$$Z(T(x)) = Z(x)$$
Let's see if that is correct. 

## Proof of Dr.Jain's claim

For $a>0$,

$$Z(ax) = \frac{ax - \mu_{ax}}{\sigma_{ax}} = \frac{ax - a\mu_{x}}{a\sigma_{x}} = Z(x)$$
and for any $c\in \mathbb{R}$,
$$Z(x + c) = \frac{x+c - \mu_{x+c}}{\sigma_{x+c}} = \frac{x+c - \mu_{x}-c}{\sigma_{x}} = Z(x)$$
Thus,
$$Z(T(x)) = Z(\frac{100}{x_{(n)} - x_{(1)}} (x-x_{(1)})) = Z(x-x_{(1)}) = Z(x)$$


## Dr. Jain's main point is CORRECT.

"If the published ‘normalized scores’ on each index are simply a 0-100 linear re-scaling of the 1-7 raw data, as BBW’s email response indicates, then the published index scores can still be standardized before applying the index weights to them."

We hope to have this:

$$ x_i \rightarrow Z(x_i) \rightarrow w_iZ(x_i) \rightarrow\sum_{i=1}^{5}w_iZ(x_i) \rightarrow T\left(\sum_{i=1}^{5}w_iZ(x_i)\right)$$
$$(1,7) \rightarrow (-3,3)\rightarrow (-3w, 3w) \rightarrow (-3, 3) \rightarrow (0, 100)$$
But, we only have scaled data (in the range of 0-100)


